1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
The 1800s was the age of the locomotive.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:08,000
It offered America power and speed for the very first time,

3
00:00:08,001 --> 00:00:11,000
and made rapid expansion into the West possible.

4
00:00:11,001 --> 00:00:14,000
But it also brought danger and death.

5
00:00:14,001 --> 00:00:16,000
In the effort to go farther and faster,

6
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:20,000
freak accidents and faulty construction claimed thousands of lives.

7
00:00:20,001 --> 00:00:24,000
Now, Trainwrecks on Modern Marvels.

8
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
The history of America's trains is one of triumph and dynamism,

9
00:00:46,700 --> 00:00:49,180
but it is also one of terror and disaster.

10
00:00:49,820 --> 00:00:51,760
By the first decade of the 20th century,

11
00:00:52,240 --> 00:00:57,100
the death toll for train-related accidents climbed to more than 12,000 people in a single year.

12
00:00:57,101 --> 00:01:00,660
peril has always been a passenger on the railroads.

13
00:01:04,100 --> 00:01:09,800
The steam locomotive was invented in England during the first three decades of the 1800s,

14
00:01:09,920 --> 00:01:14,640
but it wasn't long before businessmen in America became convinced of its profitability.

15
00:01:18,060 --> 00:01:22,480
Trains could haul more freight and travel faster than horses or boats,

16
00:01:22,481 --> 00:01:25,420
and they weren't halted by harsh winters

17
00:01:25,421 --> 00:01:28,560
when waterways often froze and roads became impassable.

18
00:01:31,820 --> 00:01:33,560
Unlike the British, however,

19
00:01:33,940 --> 00:01:37,040
early American railroad management stressed swift construction

20
00:01:37,041 --> 00:01:38,440
over safety and workmanship.

21
00:01:39,200 --> 00:01:43,760
Emphasis was placed on expansion, on new mileage.

22
00:01:44,880 --> 00:01:48,680
The motive behind all of this was money

23
00:01:48,681 --> 00:01:52,100
and so the railroads expanded just as fast

24
00:01:52,101 --> 00:01:53,380
as they possibly could.

25
00:01:53,880 --> 00:01:58,500
In many instances, the federal government subsidized this expansion.

26
00:01:59,460 --> 00:02:04,120
So as they were expanding, they were not building as carefully as they should have.

27
00:02:07,300 --> 00:02:12,180
The government granted credit and land to railroads only after track had been laid.

28
00:02:14,180 --> 00:02:16,620
The faster the companies completed track,

29
00:02:16,621 --> 00:02:20,200
the quicker they could obtain these loans and claim more real estate.

30
00:02:22,100 --> 00:02:28,140
This produced track with sharp bends, steep and bumpy grades, and flimsy construction.

31
00:02:32,800 --> 00:02:40,380
The railroad has been so badly constructed that accidents are continually happening by the cars getting off the track,

32
00:02:40,840 --> 00:02:46,600
by upsetting, and by other modes by which passengers are often injured and sometimes killed.

33
00:02:47,320 --> 00:02:48,020
J.S.

34
00:02:48,021 --> 00:02:53,160
Buckingham, an English traveler, describing his American train trip in the mid-1800s.

35
00:02:57,920 --> 00:03:04,480
In the early days of railroading, track was made by simply mounting strips of iron on top of wooden stringers.

36
00:03:04,860 --> 00:03:06,260
It was called strap rail.

37
00:03:08,500 --> 00:03:12,320
Strap rail could easily loosen and was the cause of many derailments.

38
00:03:12,321 --> 00:03:20,760
broken it was also known to ram like a spike through the bottom of a car's floor

39
00:03:20,761 --> 00:03:26,380
as a train passed over it this dangerous occurrence was called a snakehead

40
00:03:26,381 --> 00:03:33,180
the use of wood to fuel the fire in the engine created another railroading hazard

41
00:03:33,181 --> 00:03:40,280
smoke stacks from the engines regularly poured burning embers over passengers

42
00:03:40,281 --> 00:03:43,120
luggage, and property bordering the tracks.

43
00:03:45,780 --> 00:03:50,120
Often animals strayed in front of moving trains, causing them to derail.

44
00:03:52,740 --> 00:03:56,120
To end this problem, the cow catcher was invented.

45
00:03:56,680 --> 00:04:00,860
It was a characteristic feature of American locomotives never seen in Europe.

46
00:04:03,180 --> 00:04:09,320
In 1833, the John Bull became the first locomotive to be fitted with a cow catcher.

47
00:04:10,280 --> 00:04:18,480
Despite all its difficulties, the safety record of rail travel in the early 1800s was a good one.

48
00:04:18,940 --> 00:04:26,920
From about 1830 to roughly 1850 was remarkably free from serious accidents.

49
00:04:28,040 --> 00:04:31,920
There weren't very many trains running. They were short.

50
00:04:33,160 --> 00:04:36,540
Trains did not run very fast in those years either.

51
00:04:36,541 --> 00:04:40,540
15 miles an hour, maybe 18 miles an hour,

52
00:04:40,640 --> 00:04:41,720
was sort of tops.

53
00:04:44,580 --> 00:04:47,580
The first railroad engine built for commercial use

54
00:04:47,581 --> 00:04:48,940
in the United States was named

55
00:04:48,941 --> 00:04:50,800
the Best Friend of Charleston.

56
00:04:53,080 --> 00:04:55,900
It ran along a 136-mile link

57
00:04:55,901 --> 00:04:58,100
between Charleston and Hamburg, South Carolina,

58
00:04:58,360 --> 00:05:00,320
which was the longest stretch of rail

59
00:05:00,321 --> 00:05:01,460
in the world at the time.

60
00:05:02,480 --> 00:05:05,400
It was called the South Carolina Road.

61
00:05:06,540 --> 00:05:15,540
On June 17, 1831, the locomotive was pulling a train of passenger cars when the fireman

62
00:05:15,541 --> 00:05:21,540
became irritated by the loud hissing of the steam being released from the engine's boiler.

63
00:05:21,541 --> 00:05:26,540
To silence it, he forced the safety valve closed and the boiler exploded.

64
00:05:26,541 --> 00:05:33,540
The fireman became the first American killed in a train accident.

65
00:05:33,541 --> 00:05:35,648
On November 11, 1833, James C.

66
00:05:35,649 --> 00:05:47,240
Stedman became the first passenger fatality when the train he was riding on derailed after breaking an axle traveling at the considerable speed of 20 miles per hour.

67
00:05:49,880 --> 00:05:54,220
Former President John Quincy Adams was on board, but was uninjured.

68
00:05:54,221 --> 00:06:04,700
A second celebrity, Cornelius Commodore Vanderbilt, was also among the passengers who were tossed and battered in the wreck.

69
00:06:05,380 --> 00:06:10,460
Vanderbilt had amassed a fortune owning steamboats and disliked railroads immensely.

70
00:06:12,180 --> 00:06:18,240
In a strange twist of fate, Vanderbilt later became one of the greatest railroad barons of his time.

71
00:06:18,241 --> 00:06:23,620
He ruled over the New York Central Line from 1867 to 1877.

72
00:06:24,220 --> 00:06:35,220
During the first 20 years of American railroading, there were fewer than 50 fatalities.

73
00:06:35,221 --> 00:06:40,520
This gave the companies and the public a false sense of security.

74
00:06:40,521 --> 00:06:45,620
The industry's unsound construction methods remained the key ingredients in a recipe for

75
00:06:45,621 --> 00:06:46,620
disaster.

76
00:06:46,621 --> 00:06:49,740
The trains were getting longer.

77
00:06:49,741 --> 00:06:51,820
The locomotives were getting heavier.

78
00:06:51,821 --> 00:06:54,480
They were faster, and there were more of them.

79
00:06:54,820 --> 00:06:57,640
So there was a much greater chance that they were going to bump into each other.

80
00:06:57,860 --> 00:07:03,460
There was a tremendous chance that rail beds would fail under those heavier, faster loads.

81
00:07:03,461 --> 00:07:11,100
And so in 1853, the toll in passengers and railroad employees killed

82
00:07:11,101 --> 00:07:15,580
was greater than the entire 25 years of railroading up to that point.

83
00:07:15,581 --> 00:07:23,700
On January 6th, the first serious wreck of 1853 occurred.

84
00:07:24,400 --> 00:07:30,200
A noon express on the Boston and Main Line was traveling at the astonishing rate of 40 miles per hour.

85
00:07:30,720 --> 00:07:35,160
Heading to Lawrence, Massachusetts, it broke a wheel and derailed at Andover.

86
00:07:38,740 --> 00:07:44,100
There were only three fatalities, but because the train was carrying a prominent passenger,

87
00:07:44,101 --> 00:07:46,880
the incident became a major news story.

88
00:07:49,960 --> 00:07:56,160
Newspapers across the nation trumpeted headlines stating that President-elect Franklin Pierce had been killed.

89
00:07:58,520 --> 00:08:05,380
In Washington, Congress adjourned out of respect for the man who was to have become their president in a matter of days.

90
00:08:05,700 --> 00:08:08,180
But the future president actually survived.

91
00:08:08,760 --> 00:08:11,900
It was Pierce's 12-year-old son who was the victim.

92
00:08:11,901 --> 00:08:20,600
just short of two months later another fatal accident occurred in railroad operations in the

93
00:08:20,601 --> 00:08:26,520
19th century if a train was overcrowded it was sold out and there were more passengers that

94
00:08:26,521 --> 00:08:32,900
needed to travel the railroad operators or managers would simply add another train and it

95
00:08:32,901 --> 00:08:39,820
followed behind the other one uh not too far distance and this doubling up of trains on the

96
00:08:39,821 --> 00:08:44,480
same service could result in a real major accident, and it often did.

97
00:08:47,320 --> 00:08:52,440
On March 4th, the Pennsylvania Railroad added a second train to their schedule in order

98
00:08:52,441 --> 00:08:54,980
to accommodate a large number of emigrant passengers.

99
00:08:56,320 --> 00:09:00,940
During the journey, the train developed mechanical problems and was forced to stop.

100
00:09:01,740 --> 00:09:04,720
A second train suddenly slammed into it from behind.

101
00:09:04,721 --> 00:09:12,700
steam passages from the engines boiler were ruptured and many victims were scalded terribly

102
00:09:12,701 --> 00:09:18,640
seven people died it was the greatest loss of life from a train wreck up to that time

103
00:09:18,641 --> 00:09:28,720
the most infamous wreck of 1853 took place on may 6th it is considered to be the first

104
00:09:28,721 --> 00:09:30,260
Great Bridge Disaster.

105
00:09:32,620 --> 00:09:34,540
At South Norwalk, Connecticut,

106
00:09:34,920 --> 00:09:36,620
a drawbridge over the Norwalk River

107
00:09:36,621 --> 00:09:38,980
was open to allow a steamship to pass.

108
00:09:41,520 --> 00:09:43,500
A New York and New Haven train

109
00:09:43,501 --> 00:09:45,120
ran through the open drawbridge,

110
00:09:45,380 --> 00:09:46,920
plunging into the waters below.

111
00:09:47,580 --> 00:09:49,940
It had been only 300 yards short

112
00:09:49,941 --> 00:09:51,840
of its destination at Norwalk's Depot.

113
00:09:55,320 --> 00:09:57,940
The signal for safe passage over the bridge

114
00:09:57,941 --> 00:10:01,740
would have been indicated by a bright red ball hanging high from a pole.

115
00:10:02,480 --> 00:10:05,720
Witnesses later testified that the ball had been in the lowered position,

116
00:10:06,360 --> 00:10:08,620
indicating the drawbridge could not be crossed.

117
00:10:09,140 --> 00:10:11,260
The engineer should have stopped his train.

118
00:10:13,600 --> 00:10:17,280
Initially, reports were circulated that renowned poet and surgeon

119
00:10:17,281 --> 00:10:20,360
Oliver Wendell Holmes had perished in the wreckage.

120
00:10:20,860 --> 00:10:24,740
But once again, the stories of a celebrity dying proved untrue.

121
00:10:24,741 --> 00:10:30,980
Not so lucky were the 30 doctors from the American Medical Association

122
00:10:30,981 --> 00:10:34,240
who were returning by train from a convention in New York City.

123
00:10:37,560 --> 00:10:43,820
Five of their number were among the 43 victims who were crushed to death or drowned in the catastrophe.

124
00:10:45,600 --> 00:10:51,700
In New Delhi, India, 300 people were killed in a train wreck by two separate passenger lines.

125
00:10:51,701 --> 00:10:56,660
The accident occurred after the Calendi Express hit a cow on the track.

126
00:10:57,440 --> 00:10:59,820
Trainwrecks will return on Modern Marvels in a moment.

127
00:11:05,980 --> 00:11:12,300
By the middle of the 19th century, railroads were on their way to becoming the largest industry in America.

128
00:11:14,240 --> 00:11:18,700
In 1850, more than 40 manufacturers were building locomotives,

129
00:11:18,701 --> 00:11:26,280
and an estimated 3,000 steam engines were crisscrossing the countryside on 9,000 miles of track.

130
00:11:28,860 --> 00:11:31,320
Ten years later, these numbers had tripled.

131
00:11:31,760 --> 00:11:38,460
In 1860, approximately 9,000 locomotives were in use and 30,000 miles of rail had been laid.

132
00:11:39,800 --> 00:11:42,980
But safety appliances were introduced more slowly.

133
00:11:42,981 --> 00:11:50,520
The kerosene-burning headlamp became an aid to night travel.

134
00:11:51,180 --> 00:11:55,460
A swiveling set of wheels called a lead truck was placed at the front of the locomotive.

135
00:11:56,840 --> 00:12:02,160
The lead truck reduced derailments by keeping the train on the track when it was navigating curves.

136
00:12:06,400 --> 00:12:12,180
In those days, it was common for trains traveling in opposite directions to occupy the same track.

137
00:12:12,980 --> 00:12:19,980
Early railroad regulations dictated that when a conductor believed another train was approaching,

138
00:12:19,981 --> 00:12:25,980
he had to order his engineer to stop and wait in an area of track called siding.

139
00:12:29,980 --> 00:12:33,980
Only after the approaching train had passed could the order to proceed be given.

140
00:12:33,981 --> 00:12:38,980
Sometimes the wait lasted hours, with every minute costing the company money.

141
00:12:38,981 --> 00:12:47,980
In 1851, the telegraph was used for the first time to send information about the location of an oncoming train.

142
00:12:51,980 --> 00:12:57,980
The messages received by telegraph made it possible to decide whether a train could proceed safely without having to wait.

143
00:12:58,980 --> 00:13:01,980
This was a major development in rail safety.

144
00:13:02,640 --> 00:13:09,200
Unfortunately, it took many years and many lives before the telegraph was universally adopted by the railroads.

145
00:13:11,520 --> 00:13:16,500
In the meantime, the railroads used timetables to dispatch and locate trains.

146
00:13:18,380 --> 00:13:21,080
There was a difficulty with the system, however.

147
00:13:21,680 --> 00:13:23,200
Time was not yet standardized.

148
00:13:26,500 --> 00:13:30,300
Timetables were based on local time, which varied from community to community.

149
00:13:31,980 --> 00:13:34,660
When the sun was directly overhead, it was noon.

150
00:13:36,340 --> 00:13:40,420
In the state of Illinois alone, there were 27 different local times.

151
00:13:41,820 --> 00:13:43,060
Wisconsin had 38.

152
00:13:46,260 --> 00:13:52,620
It has been estimated that at one point the railroad schedule trains using as many as 100 different times.

153
00:13:53,240 --> 00:13:56,020
The confusion this caused led to many disasters.

154
00:13:56,021 --> 00:14:08,780
A crash at Camp Hill, Pennsylvania on July 17, 1856, was due to a conductor not paying strict attention to his timetable.

155
00:14:08,781 --> 00:14:16,640
It was an outing of a large extra of children who were on their way from their church in Philadelphia to a picnic.

156
00:14:17,460 --> 00:14:28,880
And along the way, their engineer disregarded timetable and ended up coming around a corner to find the scheduled train directly in front of him.

157
00:14:29,440 --> 00:14:35,680
And 67 people were killed in that wreck, mostly children, mostly burned.

158
00:14:35,681 --> 00:14:43,220
A telegraphed message relaying the positions of the two trains could have prevented the disaster.

159
00:14:46,400 --> 00:14:50,960
The conductor of the scheduled train was so distraught that before the day was out,

160
00:14:51,460 --> 00:14:54,960
he committed suicide by swallowing arsenic and morphine.

161
00:14:55,900 --> 00:14:59,140
Ironically, he was later absolved of any wrongdoing.

162
00:14:59,141 --> 00:15:07,140
Instead, a jury placed the blame on the conductor of the train carrying the children.

163
00:15:07,141 --> 00:15:11,140
His train was 23 minutes late at the time of the Camp Hill wreck.

164
00:15:11,141 --> 00:15:18,140
In fact, throughout the history of railroads, human error has always been the major cause of train disasters.

165
00:15:18,141 --> 00:15:23,140
The engineers were made into heroes because they ran the biggest, most important machines in this country.

166
00:15:23,141 --> 00:15:23,992
in this country.

167
00:15:23,993 --> 00:15:27,720
In fact, the engineers were not all that different than young men with

168
00:15:27,721 --> 00:15:30,320
their sports cars today who drive them too fast.

169
00:15:33,220 --> 00:15:36,449
America's most famous engineer was Casey Jones.

170
00:15:36,450 --> 00:15:39,540
Casey, whose real name was John Luther Jones,

171
00:15:39,541 --> 00:15:42,100
was an engineer for the Illinois Central Line.

172
00:15:45,640 --> 00:15:52,180
On the night of April 30, 1900, Casey died trying to make up time on a run from Memphis,

173
00:15:52,181 --> 00:15:52,881
Tennessee.

174
00:15:52,882 --> 00:15:57,860
The wreck occurred when he rounded a curve near Vaughan, Mississippi and saw a freight

175
00:15:57,861 --> 00:16:00,063
train on the track in front of him.

176
00:16:00,064 --> 00:16:03,840
Casey told his firemen to jump, then grabbed the whistle and

177
00:16:03,841 --> 00:16:09,681
brakes and rode the train into history and song.

178
00:16:09,682 --> 00:16:15,400
The railroad didn't like Casey very much. Casey

179
00:16:15,401 --> 00:16:20,191
wrecked one of their locomotives. He was typical of the engineers of that period.

180
00:16:20,192 --> 00:16:20,960
The important

181
00:16:20,961 --> 00:16:24,674
thing was to get the train over the road, make up time.

182
00:16:24,675 --> 00:16:27,240
And if you needed to sort of skirt the

183
00:16:27,241 --> 00:16:29,834
rules a little bit, then you do that.

184
00:16:29,835 --> 00:16:33,620
But that shows you the attitude of both railroad labor

185
00:16:33,621 --> 00:16:35,452
and railroad management in those days.

186
00:16:35,453 --> 00:16:37,960
If you could get away with it, management loved you.

187
00:16:38,080 --> 00:16:41,620
If you didn't get away with it, you were dead, and they wrote you off.

188
00:16:44,100 --> 00:16:49,340
The overwhelming majority of railroad fatalities have been employees, not passengers.

189
00:16:50,960 --> 00:16:56,460
Train crews often worked hard long shifts. Stories of wrecks caused by a

190
00:16:56,461 --> 00:17:01,620
sleeping engineer behind the throttle were common. The engineer and firemen

191
00:17:01,621 --> 00:17:09,640
were always the first ones to die in a head-on collision. Because the passenger

192
00:17:09,641 --> 00:17:14,420
cars were made of wood, fire was another cause of injury and death in 19th century

193
00:17:14,421 --> 00:17:15,877
train accidents.

194
00:17:15,878 --> 00:17:22,340
They were heated with coal stoves so that in any kind of serious wreck,

195
00:17:22,460 --> 00:17:28,760
a derailment of any sort, the car would tip and the coal stove would tip over, scattering these

196
00:17:28,761 --> 00:17:31,055
red hot coals all over the place.

197
00:17:31,056 --> 00:17:35,020
People were trapped in the wreckage, couldn't get out and

198
00:17:35,021 --> 00:17:36,260
burned up.

199
00:17:40,980 --> 00:17:43,260
One of the worst train fires occurred

200
00:17:43,261 --> 00:17:45,060
on a dreary December 18th

201
00:17:45,061 --> 00:17:46,420
in 1867.

202
00:17:52,140 --> 00:17:54,480
A service known as the New York Express

203
00:17:54,481 --> 00:17:57,080
was traveling eastbound along the eastern shore

204
00:17:57,081 --> 00:17:59,020
of Lake Erie at Angola, New York.

205
00:17:59,520 --> 00:18:01,020
Just as the train was nearing

206
00:18:01,021 --> 00:18:03,060
a bridge which spanned a frozen creek,

207
00:18:03,061 --> 00:18:06,640
a faulty axle caused the last car to derail.

208
00:18:07,900 --> 00:18:12,720
With many passengers on board, the car was dragged sideways along the bridge.

209
00:18:13,280 --> 00:18:15,780
It eventually uncoupled from the rest of the train

210
00:18:15,781 --> 00:18:19,820
and careened 40 feet down the slope toward the creek.

211
00:18:22,800 --> 00:18:27,640
When the car struck the bottom of the ravine, the two stoves inside broke apart.

212
00:18:28,360 --> 00:18:32,380
Searing hot coal spilled out over the wooden car and its occupants.

213
00:18:33,060 --> 00:18:39,740
The icy slope made it impossible for rescuers to reach the victims in time.

214
00:18:40,460 --> 00:18:42,900
Forty-two passengers perished at Angola.

215
00:18:44,280 --> 00:18:50,420
In the annals of train disasters, the wreck of the New York Express is remembered as the Angola Horror.

216
00:18:52,640 --> 00:18:59,240
In the aftermath of Angola and other catastrophes, a 21-year-old inventor named George Westinghouse

217
00:18:59,241 --> 00:19:02,320
increased his efforts to develop a new braking system.

218
00:19:03,060 --> 00:19:07,060
The brakes would be operated entirely by the engineer from the cab of the locomotive.

219
00:19:10,540 --> 00:19:14,060
A year and a half later, Westinghouse patented his airbrake.

220
00:19:14,440 --> 00:19:17,820
It was the single most important advancement in railroad safety.

221
00:19:18,900 --> 00:19:21,880
In years to come, it would save countless lives.

222
00:19:21,881 --> 00:19:33,100
The 1869 advent of the airbrake meant fewer train disasters were a real possibility.

223
00:19:35,600 --> 00:19:39,080
Railroad workers, however, were hesitant to accept the airbrake,

224
00:19:39,380 --> 00:19:44,360
because brakemen could lose their jobs if the engineer controlled all the train's brakes by himself.

225
00:19:46,600 --> 00:19:51,280
Railroad management was reluctant to convert the industry from one standard to another

226
00:19:51,281 --> 00:19:53,220
because of the enormous cost involved.

227
00:19:54,320 --> 00:19:56,360
It took years of disastrous accidents

228
00:19:56,361 --> 00:19:59,640
before air brakes were widely used by the railroads.

229
00:20:02,220 --> 00:20:05,900
The telegraph was another safety device available at the time,

230
00:20:06,240 --> 00:20:08,140
but not always used by the railroads.

231
00:20:08,620 --> 00:20:11,640
A telegraphed message could have prevented many terrible accidents,

232
00:20:12,120 --> 00:20:15,460
including one on August 26, 1871.

233
00:20:15,461 --> 00:20:16,261
button.

234
00:20:16,262 --> 00:20:25,296
That day, rail traffic had been particularly heavy to and from the Boston area.

235
00:20:25,297 --> 00:20:26,440
The number

236
00:20:26,441 --> 00:20:34,100
of regularly scheduled trains had been increased from 152 to 192 in order to accommodate passengers

237
00:20:34,101 --> 00:20:38,057
attending two revival meetings and a military gathering.

238
00:20:38,058 --> 00:20:40,600
A local train was pulling out of the

239
00:20:40,601 --> 00:20:45,820
Revere Depot when suddenly an Eastern Railroad Express slammed into it from the rear.

240
00:20:47,260 --> 00:20:52,580
Many passengers were killed instantly, while others were scorched by the boiler's escaping

241
00:20:52,581 --> 00:20:53,281
steam.

242
00:20:53,282 --> 00:20:58,780
When the fire that ensued was finally extinguished, the death toll had reached 32.

243
00:21:05,440 --> 00:21:10,440
Throughout the Civil War, the telegraph had proven itself effective in preventing accidents,

244
00:21:10,600 --> 00:21:16,600
At Revere, a telegraphed message could have warned the engineers that they were sharing the track with other trains.

245
00:21:17,740 --> 00:21:22,640
Eastern Railroad's reluctance to adopt the telegraph cost the victims their lives.

246
00:21:26,160 --> 00:21:33,020
But ultimately, disasters such as the accident at Revere forced the railroads to improve safety conditions.

247
00:21:33,021 --> 00:21:42,500
the 1871 revere wreck resulted in public indignation meetings uh it was put down to

248
00:21:42,501 --> 00:21:49,100
deliberate murder by the the clergy in the pulpits and in massachusetts charles francis adams who was

249
00:21:49,101 --> 00:21:53,940
one of the commissioners of the massachusetts state railroad commission actually went as far

250
00:21:53,941 --> 00:21:58,420
as to say that the the people who are responsible for running a single track railroad without using

251
00:21:58,421 --> 00:22:03,300
the telegraph for dispatching should be criminally liable for what happens on that railroad

252
00:22:06,980 --> 00:22:11,540
the revere crash was only one of many train disasters which captured the imagination of

253
00:22:11,541 --> 00:22:18,500
the public and the press of the day from the 1850s on there are a great number of accidents

254
00:22:18,501 --> 00:22:25,540
horrible accidents these were reported in the press of the time the daily newspapers in the

255
00:22:25,541 --> 00:22:28,899
journals such as Harper's or Leslie's and Ballou's.

256
00:22:28,900 --> 00:22:31,600
They followed every accident very closely

257
00:22:31,601 --> 00:22:36,280
and reported on these in the most sensational kind of journalism.

258
00:22:38,780 --> 00:22:44,100
The descriptions of chaos seen at train wrecks were accompanied by illustrations showing bodies

259
00:22:44,101 --> 00:22:48,320
flying through the air and lying in agony at the side of the tracks.

260
00:22:48,321 --> 00:22:57,380
By 1875, journals began to report the number of wrecks.

261
00:22:58,020 --> 00:23:03,400
The Railroad Gazette established that 1,201 accidents had occurred that year.

262
00:23:03,960 --> 00:23:06,820
This included 104 head-on collisions.

263
00:23:07,820 --> 00:23:13,780
A national census report, however, put the total much higher at 8,216.

264
00:23:14,520 --> 00:23:17,040
This meant, depending on which article someone read,

265
00:23:17,520 --> 00:23:21,420
there were at least three and as many as 22 train wrecks every day.

266
00:23:21,780 --> 00:23:23,760
But the worst was yet to come.

267
00:23:29,740 --> 00:23:35,940
The quality of bridge construction in the 1800s was as inconsistent as any other part of the track.

268
00:23:36,340 --> 00:23:41,560
Although some strong stone viaducts of the period continue to carry rail traffic even today,

269
00:23:41,561 --> 00:23:48,560
Early wooden and iron bridges could be flimsy and became the scenes of numerous rail accidents.

270
00:23:54,800 --> 00:24:01,600
The infamous Angola horror of 1867 had involved a train wreck on a bridge along the Lake Shore line.

271
00:24:02,300 --> 00:24:07,080
Nine years later, another bridge on the Lake Shore line would once again be a scene of tragedy.

272
00:24:07,700 --> 00:24:11,260
But this catastrophe proved to be even more disastrous than Angola.

273
00:24:11,560 --> 00:24:21,980
It happened a quarter of a mile east of the depot at Ashtabula, Ohio, not far from Lake

274
00:24:21,981 --> 00:24:22,681
Erie.

275
00:24:25,360 --> 00:24:32,200
On the night of December 29th, 1876, the Pacific Express was fighting its way through a violent

276
00:24:32,201 --> 00:24:35,820
gale, which raged from Lake Erie across northeastern Ohio.

277
00:24:37,580 --> 00:24:40,060
The train's destination was Chicago.

278
00:24:41,560 --> 00:24:49,620
The train's two locomotives and 11 cars were crossing the bridge over frozen Ashtabula Creek

279
00:24:49,621 --> 00:24:55,200
when suddenly the engineer heard a terrible crack and felt the track sink below him.

280
00:24:57,380 --> 00:25:02,500
Instinctively, he forced the throttle wide open, hoping to outrace a terrible fate.

281
00:25:04,120 --> 00:25:09,940
He had barely enough time, however, to look back and witness the other engine and all 11 cars

282
00:25:09,941 --> 00:25:13,480
plunge 75 feet down into the abyss.

283
00:25:15,380 --> 00:25:17,620
In spite of the roaring storm,

284
00:25:18,200 --> 00:25:21,060
Ashtabula's townspeople could hear the horrendous crash

285
00:25:21,061 --> 00:25:22,320
and came to the rescue.

286
00:25:22,860 --> 00:25:25,720
But in the confusion and rush to pull victims

287
00:25:25,721 --> 00:25:28,220
from the mountain of splintered wood and twisted iron,

288
00:25:28,840 --> 00:25:31,580
available firefighting equipment was left unmanned.

289
00:25:35,000 --> 00:25:37,300
The inevitable blaze that followed

290
00:25:37,301 --> 00:25:40,140
claimed those who had not already died on impact.

291
00:25:43,540 --> 00:25:46,160
52 passengers lived through the ordeal.

292
00:25:46,960 --> 00:25:48,840
Many were taken to the Hotel Antler

293
00:25:48,841 --> 00:25:50,360
where they received medical attention.

294
00:25:51,240 --> 00:25:53,380
The official death toll was 83.

295
00:25:54,100 --> 00:25:55,800
But all the survivors agreed

296
00:25:55,801 --> 00:25:58,840
that as many as 70 more people were missing in the wreckage.

297
00:26:02,300 --> 00:26:05,460
An investigation found that faulty bridge construction

298
00:26:05,461 --> 00:26:07,180
caused the disaster at Ashtabula.

299
00:26:07,300 --> 00:26:09,700
Blame was placed on those who had built it.

300
00:26:13,860 --> 00:26:17,340
Charles Collins, an engineer for the Lakeshore Railroad,

301
00:26:17,460 --> 00:26:20,280
had questioned the bridge's construction when it was being built.

302
00:26:20,820 --> 00:26:25,740
But the man in charge, Amasa Stone, gave orders to have it erected anyway.

303
00:26:26,440 --> 00:26:28,600
Collins then did his best to make it safe.

304
00:26:28,601 --> 00:26:38,040
Because of their prominent positions, neither man was prosecuted.

305
00:26:38,041 --> 00:26:45,060
But two days after giving testimony, Charles Collins was found dead from a self-inflicted gunshot wound.

306
00:26:45,760 --> 00:26:49,660
Five years later, Amasa Stone also took his own life.

307
00:26:49,661 --> 00:26:59,160
In the wake of Ashtabula and other disasters, something had to be done to make train travel more efficient and safer.

308
00:27:02,340 --> 00:27:07,840
A meeting of railroad superintendents known as the General Time Convention was held in Chicago.

309
00:27:08,660 --> 00:27:12,420
There, the decision was made to adopt standard railroad time.

310
00:27:12,421 --> 00:27:21,000
A little over a month later, on November 18, 1883, Standard Time went into effect.

311
00:27:21,001 --> 00:27:29,740
It divided the country into four zones, each one an hour apart, and it eliminated more than 100 local railroad times.

312
00:27:36,100 --> 00:27:40,040
Other safety measures were also being adopted in the 1880s.

313
00:27:40,041 --> 00:27:42,360
More trains were using air brakes.

314
00:27:42,880 --> 00:27:46,380
Dangerous coal stoves were being replaced by steam heating systems.

315
00:27:47,140 --> 00:27:50,840
Old and brittle iron trap was being converted to stronger steel.

316
00:27:51,440 --> 00:27:57,240
And a device called the torpedo signal was placed on tracks and burst when a train ran over it.

317
00:27:57,780 --> 00:28:02,040
This signal warned engineers that a disabled train blocked the track ahead.

318
00:28:03,540 --> 00:28:07,400
For the first time, improved railroad safety seemed possible.

319
00:28:07,401 --> 00:28:11,540
But in reality, things were going to worsen before they got better.

320
00:28:16,680 --> 00:28:26,760
On a hot, dry August 10th in 1887, an excursion train from Peoria, Illinois, was carrying 600 holiday-bound vacationers to Niagara Falls.

321
00:28:26,761 --> 00:28:38,040
At a quarter to midnight, the train was passing a number of culverts three miles beyond the town of Chatsworth when an engineer noticed a flickering light ahead.

322
00:28:38,880 --> 00:28:45,600
The railroad crews had been out burning weeds earlier in the day and had inadvertently set one of these culverts on fire.

323
00:28:45,980 --> 00:28:54,840
The engineer is moving along with his train and he sees fire in the distance and then begins to realize that in fact it is the bridge he's about to cross.

324
00:28:56,760 --> 00:29:02,840
The bridge held long enough for the first locomotive to cross before it collapsed,

325
00:29:03,260 --> 00:29:08,080
taking the second locomotive and nine of the train's 15 wooden cars with it.

326
00:29:08,640 --> 00:29:11,120
82 passengers died in the wreckage.

327
00:29:11,760 --> 00:29:16,280
Most of the victims were crushed as each car thrust itself into the one in front of it.

328
00:29:16,281 --> 00:29:24,120
The premier among the horrors of traveling in 19th century railroads was something we call telescoping.

329
00:29:24,121 --> 00:29:31,540
It results when a car is crushed and pushed ahead violently into the car ahead of it,

330
00:29:31,720 --> 00:29:35,940
like the sections of a telescope would if you were to collapse it.

331
00:29:36,340 --> 00:29:40,140
In such an accident, it was about 100% fatalities.

332
00:29:42,360 --> 00:29:46,960
The devastation caused by telescoping at Chatsworth never would have happened

333
00:29:46,961 --> 00:29:51,440
if the railroad crew burning weeds had made sure their fires had been extinguished.

334
00:29:54,120 --> 00:29:59,540
Another oversight by a railroad employee was the cause of a runaway train wreck in 1893.

335
00:30:01,760 --> 00:30:03,778
During the late 1800s, Walter L.

336
00:30:03,779 --> 00:30:08,320
Main was the proprietor of one of the largest train circuses in America.

337
00:30:09,280 --> 00:30:12,380
Among the troupe of 300 were midgets and acrobats,

338
00:30:12,820 --> 00:30:17,400
who performed gymnastic feats astride many of the show's 130 horses.

339
00:30:17,401 --> 00:30:25,400
The circus also featured a menagerie which included elephants, lions, a zebra, and other exotic animals.

340
00:30:29,400 --> 00:30:38,400
On the night of May 29th, the circus train was winding down the eastern slopes of the Allegheny Mountains toward the town of Tyrone when its brakes began to fail.

341
00:30:38,401 --> 00:30:44,400
to fail realizing his train was out of control the engineer began to blow the whistle to warn

342
00:30:44,401 --> 00:30:48,160
the members of the circus and local inhabitants of a possible crash

343
00:30:50,880 --> 00:30:57,520
an s-shaped curve at a point called mccann's crossing proved to be too sharp car after car

344
00:30:57,521 --> 00:31:05,440
flipped off the track and plunged down a 40-foot embankment when the roar of the crash subsided

345
00:31:05,441 --> 00:31:10,540
Five people were dead, and the bodies of 60 horses were strewn along the hillside.

346
00:31:11,060 --> 00:31:13,060
Other animals broke free and escaped.

347
00:31:13,900 --> 00:31:16,500
Hannah Friday went out to the barnyard to milk the cows.

348
00:31:16,760 --> 00:31:22,560
She saw a shadow out of the corner of her eye, and it was a Bengal tiger in full charge of her and the cow.

349
00:31:23,120 --> 00:31:26,840
She leaped from her milking stool and ran to the safety of the home,

350
00:31:27,020 --> 00:31:31,300
and her and her family could only watch as the Bengal tiger made breakfast of their cow.

351
00:31:31,301 --> 00:31:40,260
Before descending down the mountainside, a railroad superintendent had determined the train had sufficient braking power.

352
00:31:43,080 --> 00:31:48,720
However, he was never told that the train's cars were twice the length and weight of a normal car.

353
00:31:49,180 --> 00:31:55,620
Had he been aware of the potential problem, he would have increased the train's braking power by adding a second locomotive,

354
00:31:55,621 --> 00:32:00,220
and the Walter L. Main Circus train most likely never would have derailed.

355
00:32:01,300 --> 00:32:09,800
At the turn of the century, safety benefits gained through new regulations, and better equipment were constantly being canceled out by other forces.

356
00:32:09,801 --> 00:32:17,580
Better track meant railroads could run faster trains

357
00:32:17,581 --> 00:32:23,160
The gradual implementation of stronger steel cars meant more freight was hauled

358
00:32:23,161 --> 00:32:27,480
Greater efficiency gained by scheduling trains using standard time

359
00:32:27,481 --> 00:32:31,700
meant management ran more trains without providing more track

360
00:32:31,701 --> 00:32:35,660
Therefore, wrecks continued to occur on a daily basis

361
00:32:35,661 --> 00:32:43,000
In 1890, there had been 6,335 railroad fatalities.

362
00:32:43,880 --> 00:32:51,120
But in 1917, over 10,000 people were killed working on, struck by, or riding American trains.

363
00:32:51,620 --> 00:32:57,240
The following year, 1918, the death toll reached more than 9,000.

364
00:32:59,240 --> 00:33:04,840
The worst train disaster in American history took place on July 9th in 1918.

365
00:33:05,660 --> 00:33:13,400
Minutes after departing from Nashville, Tennessee, a northbound train was scheduled to pass a

366
00:33:13,401 --> 00:33:18,180
southbound service from Memphis along a stretch of double track.

367
00:33:18,181 --> 00:33:26,780
That particular morning, the Memphis train did not appear as scheduled.

368
00:33:26,781 --> 00:33:31,240
The northbound engineer should have stopped before the double track merged into a single

369
00:33:31,241 --> 00:33:34,680
track to wait until the southbound service had passed.

370
00:33:34,681 --> 00:33:37,780
it would have been safe for him to use the single track northward.

371
00:33:38,400 --> 00:33:42,660
For reasons unknown to this day, the engineer did not wait.

372
00:33:43,400 --> 00:33:49,240
A half a mile further along the line, he came around a blind curve, and before he had time

373
00:33:49,241 --> 00:33:55,340
to apply the brakes, the trains smashed into one another at a combined impact speed of

374
00:33:55,341 --> 00:33:57,060
100 miles per hour.

375
00:33:57,061 --> 00:34:03,560
Initial estimates put the number of dead at 121

376
00:34:03,561 --> 00:34:06,800
But the official count eventually reached 101

377
00:34:06,801 --> 00:34:11,100
There were also 171 others who were injured

378
00:34:11,101 --> 00:34:15,280
Most victims were World War I gunpowder factory workers

379
00:34:15,281 --> 00:34:20,900
An investigation of the crash concluded

380
00:34:20,901 --> 00:34:23,720
That the number of casualties at the Nashville train wreck

381
00:34:23,721 --> 00:34:27,340
would have been far fewer if the cars had been made of steel.

382
00:34:28,280 --> 00:34:31,000
The steel car was invented in the 19th century,

383
00:34:31,200 --> 00:34:35,260
but it didn't come into common usage on railways for a long time,

384
00:34:35,360 --> 00:34:37,120
and when it did come into use,

385
00:34:38,000 --> 00:34:42,160
it was mainly used for the more luxury cars, the Pullmans,

386
00:34:42,161 --> 00:34:46,040
the cars that the well-to-do of the day would ride in.

387
00:34:46,380 --> 00:34:49,480
In the 1918 wreck, most of the people who died

388
00:34:49,481 --> 00:34:53,120
were among the poorer folks who were riding in the wooden coaches.

389
00:34:53,720 --> 00:35:01,240
It is believed the responsibility for the disaster belonged to the conductor and engineer of the northbound train out of Nashville.

390
00:35:01,980 --> 00:35:05,620
Because they disregarded the rules, the victims paid the price.

391
00:35:10,500 --> 00:35:18,060
The standard length for a passenger car is 85 feet, but freight trains can run from 24 feet to 300 feet.

392
00:35:18,740 --> 00:35:20,220
Modern Marvels will return in a moment.

393
00:35:23,720 --> 00:35:30,820
The heart of a steam locomotive is its boiler.

394
00:35:31,120 --> 00:35:38,920
If the proper balance of fire and water is maintained, the boiler will provide a steady supply of high-pressure steam to the engine cylinders.

395
00:35:39,700 --> 00:35:44,880
The pistons inside the cylinders then force the drive wheels to turn and the train moves.

396
00:35:46,420 --> 00:35:51,240
If, however, the water level is allowed to drop too far, the boiler will overheat.

397
00:35:51,241 --> 00:35:57,880
Its metal walls will soften and crack, and it will explode with enormous force.

398
00:36:01,900 --> 00:36:05,560
Relatively few train accidents were the result of boiler explosions.

399
00:36:06,380 --> 00:36:12,800
But one such accident did occur at a workshop in San Antonio, Texas, on March 18, 1912.

400
00:36:13,560 --> 00:36:17,120
A locomotive that had been overhauled was in the process of being tested.

401
00:36:17,121 --> 00:36:24,360
Its boiler suddenly burst and the engine, as well as several surrounding buildings, were completely destroyed.

402
00:36:26,840 --> 00:36:33,160
One 16,000-pound chunk of metal came down nearly a quarter of a mile away from the blast site.

403
00:36:33,720 --> 00:36:37,080
A nearby house was smashed by a huge piece of the engine.

404
00:36:37,960 --> 00:36:41,420
28 people were killed and 40 more were injured.

405
00:36:41,421 --> 00:36:49,460
An official finding stated that excessive pressure due to low water level in the boiler caused the explosion.

406
00:36:54,460 --> 00:36:59,320
Another boiler explosion happened in 1934 near Powleton, West Virginia.

407
00:37:00,100 --> 00:37:08,080
On December 27th, employees of the Elkhorn Piney Coal Mining Company and their families were returning home on the company's train.

408
00:37:12,100 --> 00:37:15,460
Without warning, the engine's boiler exploded.

409
00:37:16,180 --> 00:37:20,980
After being thrown into the air, it came down on top of the first coach of the train.

410
00:37:21,780 --> 00:37:25,180
Seventeen passengers were crushed and died under its weight.

411
00:37:25,900 --> 00:37:28,900
An additional 43 people were severely injured.

412
00:37:32,780 --> 00:37:39,400
By the end of the 1930s, the transition from steam to diesel-powered locomotives had just begun.

413
00:37:41,420 --> 00:37:46,620
In 1939, one of the most notorious train wrecks in American history

414
00:37:46,621 --> 00:37:51,680
involved a diesel-electric streamliner known as the City of San Francisco.

415
00:37:55,320 --> 00:38:00,000
Owned by Union Pacific, it was among the most beautiful trains of its day,

416
00:38:00,320 --> 00:38:04,900
and it could make the 2,263-mile journey from Chicago, Illinois,

417
00:38:04,901 --> 00:38:08,420
to Oakland, California, in just under 40 hours.

418
00:38:11,420 --> 00:38:17,720
At 9.30 on the night of August 12th, the streamliner had just passed through Carlin, Nevada, heading west.

419
00:38:21,340 --> 00:38:31,011
Gliding along at 60 miles per hour, it started to cross a bridge over the Humboldt River when all of the sudden, the passenger cars began to derail and break loose

420
00:38:31,012 --> 00:38:32,020
from the engines.

421
00:38:35,460 --> 00:38:39,040
One after another, they crashed into the riverbed below.

422
00:38:39,820 --> 00:38:43,600
24 dead passengers lay buried inside the mountain of wreckage.

423
00:38:44,220 --> 00:38:47,200
115 others had sustained injuries.

424
00:38:49,480 --> 00:38:53,860
By morning, an investigation into the cause of the crash was underway.

425
00:38:54,620 --> 00:38:57,460
It quickly became clear that it was sabotage.

426
00:38:59,400 --> 00:39:04,280
The cars had jumped the track because a saboteur had removed a number of spikes

427
00:39:04,281 --> 00:39:06,380
and then pulled a rail out of alignment.

428
00:39:06,381 --> 00:39:13,540
A search of the river uncovered tools which were believed to have been used to move the displaced rail.

429
00:39:15,280 --> 00:39:20,100
They were typical tools used by railroad workers working on the track.

430
00:39:20,420 --> 00:39:23,300
So there was an immediate suspicion of a disgruntled worker.

431
00:39:24,160 --> 00:39:32,340
The investigation of this wreck took on almost a passion among the law enforcement people, the railroad people.

432
00:39:33,260 --> 00:39:35,180
They wanted to find who did this.

433
00:39:36,380 --> 00:39:44,620
Eventually, 1,144 major leads were investigated without luck.

434
00:39:47,120 --> 00:39:52,980
The railroad continued its investigation well into the 1950s, but although they believed

435
00:39:52,981 --> 00:39:57,720
the perpetrator of the crime was one of their own employees, the person who caused the wreck

436
00:39:57,721 --> 00:40:00,460
of the city of San Francisco was never caught.

437
00:40:00,461 --> 00:40:09,900
As the middle of the 20th century approached, safety measures, such as two-way radios for communication

438
00:40:09,901 --> 00:40:15,160
and automatic braking systems for stopping trains when an engineer missed a warning signal,

439
00:40:15,640 --> 00:40:18,140
were truly reducing the hazards of train travel.

440
00:40:19,360 --> 00:40:22,360
Still, disasters were not entirely eliminated.

441
00:40:22,361 --> 00:40:33,160
on february 18th 1947 the red arrow one of pennsylvania railroad's so-called blue ribbon fleet

442
00:40:33,161 --> 00:40:37,760
crashed on an allegheny mountainside 23 people perished

443
00:40:37,761 --> 00:40:44,280
the red arrow was more than an hour late on its way from detroit to new york

444
00:40:44,281 --> 00:40:50,960
when it was hurled off the track at bennington curve the interstate commerce commission found

445
00:40:50,961 --> 00:40:53,660
that excessive speed had caused the train to derail.

446
00:40:56,540 --> 00:40:58,640
Excessive speed was the cause of another,

447
00:40:58,920 --> 00:41:01,480
far more deadly derailment in 1951.

448
00:41:02,520 --> 00:41:05,420
A shambles of twisted steel near Woodbridge, New Jersey,

449
00:41:05,720 --> 00:41:08,480
is the stark picture of one of the worst railroad tragedies

450
00:41:08,481 --> 00:41:09,740
in the nation's history.

451
00:41:10,420 --> 00:41:12,660
Plunging 26 feet from a temporary trestle,

452
00:41:13,100 --> 00:41:15,480
the engine and eight cars of the cracked Broker Special

453
00:41:15,481 --> 00:41:17,120
crashed down an embankment.

454
00:41:17,700 --> 00:41:19,500
The train was carrying 1,000 passengers.

455
00:41:19,501 --> 00:41:22,840
The broker special was particularly crowded the evening of the wreck

456
00:41:22,841 --> 00:41:26,800
because a labor strike had closed down the operations of another railroad.

457
00:41:28,560 --> 00:41:32,200
Of the more than 1,000 people who were on board the train,

458
00:41:32,660 --> 00:41:36,680
84 were killed and 350 sustained injuries.

459
00:41:40,660 --> 00:41:43,580
The temporary trestle where the train derailed

460
00:41:43,581 --> 00:41:46,720
had a reduced speed limit of 25 miles per hour.

461
00:41:47,480 --> 00:41:52,360
Later testimony indicated that the engineer knew about the trestle and its reduced speed limit.

462
00:41:52,880 --> 00:41:58,720
Nevertheless, the investigation determined the train was traveling at over 50 miles per hour when it derailed.

463
00:42:02,460 --> 00:42:07,520
There is one crash that has been called the all-time sensational runaway train wreck.

464
00:42:07,521 --> 00:42:10,740
It occurred on January 15, 1953.

465
00:42:10,741 --> 00:42:16,600
Thousands of visitors were on their way to the nation's capital

466
00:42:16,601 --> 00:42:20,120
to attend the presidential inauguration of Dwight D. Eisenhower

467
00:42:20,121 --> 00:42:25,540
Some were arriving at Washington's Union Station

468
00:42:25,541 --> 00:42:28,060
on board Pennsylvania Railroad's Federal Express

469
00:42:28,061 --> 00:42:35,220
As the train approached the station, the engineer applied the brakes

470
00:42:35,221 --> 00:42:38,860
When they did not respond, he began to blast his horn

471
00:42:38,861 --> 00:42:41,460
to warn the train yard that disaster was imminent.

472
00:42:43,740 --> 00:42:47,100
There was just enough time for passengers on board the Federal

473
00:42:47,101 --> 00:42:48,800
to move to the rear of the train

474
00:42:48,801 --> 00:42:52,300
and for people in the station to run for their lives.

475
00:42:54,260 --> 00:42:57,360
Shortly thereafter, the Federal plowed into the building,

476
00:42:57,760 --> 00:43:00,820
destroying the station master's office and the main newsstand.

477
00:43:02,860 --> 00:43:04,720
After the train came to a halt,

478
00:43:04,721 --> 00:43:08,760
the station's floor gave way and the locomotive fell into the basement.

479
00:43:08,860 --> 00:43:13,200
87 people were injured in the wreck of the federal

480
00:43:13,201 --> 00:43:16,160
but amazingly not a single person was killed

481
00:43:16,161 --> 00:43:20,880
now the eisenhower inauguration was just a few days away

482
00:43:20,881 --> 00:43:24,220
what do you do when you got a locomotive sitting in the basement of your station

483
00:43:24,221 --> 00:43:27,240
and most people are going to arrive by train for the inauguration

484
00:43:27,241 --> 00:43:29,340
well you just build a floor over it

485
00:43:29,341 --> 00:43:33,880
and a lot of people arrived for the eisenhower inauguration

486
00:43:33,881 --> 00:43:36,760
and they wouldn't have been able to tell anything happened

487
00:43:36,761 --> 00:43:38,820
if it hadn't been for the newspaper stories.

488
00:43:41,940 --> 00:43:45,120
A few weeks later, the false floor was removed.

489
00:43:45,540 --> 00:43:47,900
The locomotive was lifted out and reassembled.

490
00:43:48,340 --> 00:43:52,280
It was then put back on the tracks, and it continued to run for years.

491
00:43:55,060 --> 00:43:59,820
By the 1950s, the automobile had become the American way to get around.

492
00:44:00,460 --> 00:44:03,020
People were choosing to travel on the interstate highways,

493
00:44:03,440 --> 00:44:06,040
forsaking the steel tracks of the railroads.

494
00:44:06,760 --> 00:44:10,760
Gradually, passenger trains became fewer in number.

495
00:44:10,761 --> 00:44:17,760
The airline industry finished the job of wiping out most of what was left of train passenger service.

496
00:44:17,761 --> 00:44:24,760
As the number of people riding the rails diminished, fewer passengers were killed and injured in railroad accidents.

497
00:44:24,761 --> 00:44:30,760
The era when train disasters were commonplace had come to an end.

